3. Function to Add a User:

def add_user(name, email):
    new_user = User(name=name, email=email)

    session.add(new_user)
    session.commit()  # save
    print("User added")

add_user(name, email):
This function adds a new user to the users table.
It creates a new instance of the User class with the provided name and email.

session.add(new_user):
Adds the new user to the current session, meaning SQLAlchemy will track it.

session.commit():
Commits the transaction, meaning the new user is saved to the database permanently.

The function then prints "User added" to indicate that the operation was successful.


4. Function to Delete a User:

def delete_user(id):
    # Query the user by id
    user_to_delete = session.query(User).filter_by(id=id).one()  # Fetch the user by id

    if user_to_delete:
        session.delete(user_to_delete)  # Delete the fetched user
        session.commit()  # Commit the transaction
        print("User deleted")
    else:
        print(f"User with id {id} not found")

delete_user(id):
This function deletes a user from the users table based on the provided id.

session.query(User).filter_by(id=id).one() / .first():
Fetches the user with the specified id.

session.delete(user_to_delete):
Marks the user_to_delete for deletion.

session.commit():
Commits the transaction to delete the user from the database.

If no user is found with the given id, it prints "User with id {id} not found".


5. Function to Update a User:

def update_user(name, new_email):
    user_to_update = session.query(User).filter_by(name=name).first()

    if user_to_update:
        user_to_update.email = new_email
        session.commit()  # save
        print(f"User {user_to_update.name}'s email updated to {new_email}")

update_user(name, new_email):
This function updates the email of a user based on the provided name.

session.query(User).filter_by(name=name).first():
Fetches the first user that matches the provided name.
If the user is found, it updates the email field with the new value (new_email).

session.commit():
Saves the updated user to the database.


The reason why you don’t need to explicitly use session.add() when updating an existing record
(like in update_rest()) is due to the way SQLAlchemy's ORM (Object Relational Mapper)
works with session tracking.

Here's the explanation:
Session and Object Tracking:

SQLAlchemy automatically tracks objects that are queried and returned from the database.
This means that when you fetch an object (e.g., new_data in update_rest()),
SQLAlchemy already knows that this object is tied to an existing record in the database.

When you modify a property of that object (e.g., new_data.famous_dish = famous_dish),
SQLAlchemy knows that you are modifying an already existing record and will handle it during
the commit operation.

Adding New Data:

session.add(new_rest) is used when you're inserting new data into the database.
You need to explicitly tell SQLAlchemy to track the new object (new_rest) by using session.add()
because SQLAlchemy doesn't know about this object until you add it to the session.
The session will then handle the insertion when you call session.commit().

Updating Existing Data:

In the update_rest() function, after fetching the object
with session.query(Restaurants).filter_by(name=name).first(),
the object is already tracked by the session because it was retrieved from the database.

When you change the attribute (new_data.famous_dish = famous_dish), SQLAlchemy automatically knows
that this object is being modified and will update the record in the database when you commit the session.
No need to explicitly call session.add() in this case.

SQLAlchemy only requires session.add() when you're creating new objects or new records,
because it needs to know about these objects for insertion.
But once an object is retrieved from the database (via a query),
SQLAlchemy starts tracking it automatically.


Commit:

After you modify the famous_dish attribute in update_rest(), when you call session.commit(),
SQLAlchemy will flush the changes to the database (i.e., it will generate an UPDATE SQL statement
to modify the existing record).

If you hadn't modified the object (if you had just queried it without making changes),
SQLAlchemy wouldn't perform any action during the commit.

